**DEFINITION.** Let $\mathfrak{G}$ be the class of open subsets of $\mathbf{P}\omega$, and $\mathfrak{F}$ be the class of closed subsets. Further let $\mathfrak{B}$ be the class of all (finite) Boolean combinations of open sets.

We recall from § 1 that $U \in \mathfrak{G}$ just in case for all $x \in \mathbf{P}\omega$, we have $x \in U$ if and only if some finite subset of $x$ is in $U$. The class of open sets contains $\varnothing$ and $\mathbf{P}\omega$ and is closed under finite intersection and arbitrary union; in fact, it can be generated by these two closure conditions from subsets of the special form $\{x \in \mathbf{P}\omega \mid n \in x\}$ for the various $n \in \omega$. An open set is always *monotonic* (whenever $x \in U$ and $x \subseteq y$, then $y \in U$), so that every nonempty $U \in \mathfrak{G}$ has $\top \in U$.

Another characterization of openness can be given by continuous functions. Suppose $U \in \mathfrak{G}$. Define $f : \mathbf{P}\omega \to \{\bot, \top\}$ so that

$$U = \{x \mid f(x) = \top\};$$

then $f$ is continuous. Conversely, if such an $f$ is continuous, then $U$ is open. But if we do not assume the range of $f$ is included in $\{\bot, \top\}$, this is not true. For the case of general functions we know that $f$ is continuous if and only if $\{x \mid f(x) \in V\}$ is open for all open $V$. This defines continuity in terms of openness, but we can turn it the other way around:

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`ScottF`).** $\mathfrak{F}$ is the class of closed subsets of $\mathbf{P}\omega$: those $U$ whose complement is Scott-open.

**Definition (`IsScottGdelta`).** $U$ is a $\mathfrak{G}_\delta$ when it is a countable intersection of Scott-open sets.

**Definition (`IsFcapG`).** $U$ belongs to $\mathfrak{F}\dot{\cap}\mathfrak{G}$ when $U=C\cap O$ for some closed $C$ and Scott-open $O$.

**Definition (`IsFcapGdelta`).** $U$ belongs to $\mathfrak{F}\dot{\cap}\mathfrak{G}_\delta$ when $U=C\cap G$ for some closed $C$ and $\mathfrak{G}_\delta$ set $G$.

**Definition (`IsBooleanOpen`).** The Boolean combinations of Scott-open sets are generated inductively by taking opens, binary unions, binary intersections, and complements.

**Definition (`ScottB`).** $\mathfrak{B}$ is the class of sets satisfying $\mathrm{IsBooleanOpen}$.

**Definition (`IsBdelta`).** $U$ is a $\mathfrak{B}_\delta$ when it is a countable intersection of Boolean combinations of opens.

**Theorem (`IsBooleanOpen.of_closed`).** Every closed set is Boolean.

*Proof.* The complement of a closed set is open, hence Boolean, and Boolean sets are closed under complement. ∎

**Theorem (`IsBooleanOpen.of_fcapG`).** Every set in $\mathfrak{F}\dot{\cap}\mathfrak{G}$ is Boolean.

*Proof.* Intersect a closed (hence Boolean) set with an open. ∎

**Theorem (`IsBooleanOpen.sUnion_finite`).** A finite union of Boolean sets is Boolean.

*Proof.* By induction on the finite family: the empty union is the empty open set, and binary union closes the inductive step. ∎
